"""tests/gate_census_unproven_residue.py — the #3536 per-entrant proof ledger.

THE dated, shrink-only record of every census gate that carried the `unproven`
verdict when the per-entrant rule landed (charter primitive 3, docs/CHARTER.md:
"debt is counted, exemptions are dated, and the count only moves down"). The rule
it feeds lives in tests/test_gate_census_lane_3000.py (`check_unproven_entrants`):
every gate the live census reports `unproven` must be a key here, so a gate that
is NOT here arrives with a verdict — `PROVEN_CAN_FAIL` / `ATTEMPTED_UNPROVEN` in
scripts/gate_census.py, or a `not-applicable` reason — or it does not land.

WHY A LEDGER AND NOT A NUMBER. The #3000 ratchet was a COUNT ceiling
(`BASELINE_UNPROVEN_GATES`, 541) over a live pile of 536–538, and a count has
two holes the 2026-09-05 forensic RCA named in one line — "prove one, mint one":
the 16 of headroom let a new guard enter unproven with nothing said, and even at
zero headroom a PR that proves one old gate can mint one new unproven gate for
free. Keyed by gate, neither move is silent: the only way a new unproven gate
passes is a NEW LINE IN THIS FILE, in the diff, with a date and a reason.

KEYS. A gate's key is `stable_key()` in the lane test: the census id for every
family except `ci`, whose ids are POSITIONAL (`ci::<wf>::<job>::<index>` — inserting
a step slides every later id, which is why the #3000 ratchet was a count). A CI
step is keyed on its workflow path + `<job> / <step label>` instead, so inserting
a step ahead of it does not make it a new entrant; RENAMING a step does, and that
is the honest reading — a relabelled step is a decision someone made on the PR.

SEAL. Populated by `python3 scripts/gate_census.py --json` on main at ea41f094b
(2026-09-05): 597 gates, 538 `unproven`, every one of them a line below. Two more
lines carry PRs that were already open and measured on their own branch exports
when the seal was taken — named by PR so their merge is the line's exit, not a
retroactive refusal (the rule gates ENTRANTS from the point it lands; it is not a
block on work that predates it).

DIRECTION. Entries only ever come OUT: prove the gate (a MutationSpec or a
gate_census_proofs record), record the attempt, or retire the gate, then delete
its line. `UNPROVEN_CEILING_HIGH_WATER` in the lane test holds the count; a line
that no longer matches a live unproven gate is printed by name on every run
(`RATCHET DOWN AVAILABLE`, non-fatal per the #3329 owner decision) so the delete
is never a surprise. Adding a line is the one act this file exists to make
visible — it is a `# gate-entrypoint:`-grade decision, never a lane's convenience.
"""

